1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,900
Not Blocked. Forgotten.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:33,968
Sarah Callahan sat in the bathtub of a windowless bathroom, writing her story. The satellite phone rang. The second mate of a tanker anchored deep in the Gulf. From Kerala, India, twenty-nine. “The northern horizon’s red. That’s toward Kharg.” “What do you have left on board?” “Eleven days of water. Vegetables ran out. That’s not the problem. We’re sitting on two million barrels of oil, ma’am.”

3
00:00:34,568 --> 00:01:02,404
“Every night you hear drones. Every time one goes over, twenty-three men stop breathing. Every day I write my mother a text. I can’t send it.” Sarah took out her notebook. “People write that the strait is blocked. We are not blocked. We are forgotten. Please write it that way.” On the map of a war only the warships are drawn. The ships between them, on nobody’s side, are not.

4
00:01:03,004 --> 00:01:32,352
The cable from Seoul was one line. Report on-scene assessment. Lt. Kim Junho knew what it was asking. The American president had told South Korea, too, to send warships. He stood at the whiteboard. On the left, the ships hit near the strait in two weeks. On the right, the two Indian ships that went through yesterday. The strait was not closed. It had a doorman. Ships from countries that could talk to Tehran got through.

5
00:01:32,952 --> 00:02:00,044
He wrote the report. First paragraph, facts. Second, the Indian case. In the third his hand stopped. But his father had said a soldier who loses the strength to think is no soldier. A warship can escort a ship. It cannot bring out a ship already trapped. He read it three times and sent it. His hands shook. The first time he had put his own name behind an opinion.

6
00:02:00,644 --> 00:02:04,432
Epic Fury continues in Episode 6.
